research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-72-{"id":"def-uniform-cover-space","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"The definition of a uniform-cover structure is mathematically correct and well-formed: it defines the star operator, star-refinement, and the three axioms (upward closure under coarsening, common refinement, star-refinement) with nonemptiness, matching the standard formulation. The cross-reference to the entourage-cover dictionary lemma is faithful: that lemma establishes the equivalence between cover and entourage formulations and connects cover stars to entourage balls, through which the induced topology is obtained. No false claims, mis-cited steps, or logical gaps found.","at":"2026-07-30T15:03:02.262Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-73-{"id":"ex-arens-space-is-sequential-not-frechet-urysohn","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"Verified that step 1.1 correctly shows S_2 is not Fréchet–Urysohn (infinity lies in the closure of A but no sequence in A converges to it, with both the infinite-row-visit and finite-row-visit cases handled), and steps 1.2–2.2 correctly show S_2 is sequential (isolated points, row centres, and infinity are all shown to lie in any sequentially closed C containing their closure). All [L1], [L2], and [A1] citations faithfully restate their sources. There is a minor 30-second gap in step 1.2: the contradiction argument for 'infinitely many x_n in C' when infinity is in the closure of C implicitly assumes infinity is not already in C (otherwise the constructed neighbourhood still contains infinity and meets C), but the conclusion holds trivially in that case, and a competent reader closes this immediately.","at":"2026-07-30T15:03:33.653Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-74-{"id":"lem-metric-uniformity-dictionary","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"Verified each proof step against its cited facts: step 1.1 correctly derives diagonal containment, symmetry, intersection refinement, and the square-root property from metric axioms via [L1]; step 2.1 correctly applies [L4] to get a filter and then checks the three uniformity axioms from def-uniform-space-by-entourages, and correctly identifies entourage balls with metric balls to match the topology via [L2] and [L5]; step 2.2 correctly proves separatedness using the metric separation axiom; step 3.1 correctly identifies the basic-entourage uniform-continuity condition with the metric epsilon-delta condition of [L3]. All [L#] facts faithfully restate their cited definitions. The title matches what is proved. No contradictions with siblings or companion items found.","at":"2026-07-30T15:03:44.448Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-75-{"id":"def-gauge-of-pseudometrics","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"The definition correctly defines a gauge of pseudometrics, the entourages E(F,epsilon), and the generated uniformity. The filter base claim is valid: each E(F,epsilon) contains the diagonal (so is nonempty when X is), intersections are refined by E(F1∪F2, min(eps1,eps2)), and the family is nonempty. The generated filter satisfies the uniformity axioms (diagonal containment, symmetry from pseudometric symmetry, square root via epsilon/2 and the triangle inequality). The definition of uniform continuity for a pseudometric and the claim that each gauge member is uniformly continuous for the generated uniformity are both correct. Citations to def-filter-base and lem-filter-base-generates provide the filter-base framework; the uniformity verification is routine and the claims are true.","at":"2026-07-30T15:04:22.024Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl:76:{"id":"lem-entourage-and-uniform-cover-dictionary","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"Verified the proof step by step: step 1.1 correctly constructs star-refinements using iterated symmetric square roots from [L1]; step 1.2 correctly verifies the entourage axioms from uniform-cover axioms via [L2]; steps 2.1–2.3 correctly establish mutual inverseness of the two constructions using the inclusions D ⊆ E_{C_D} ⊆ D∘2 and the identity E_V[x] = St(x,V). Citations to the two definitions and the symmetric-base lemma are faithful, and the [L1] compound fact (symmetric entourages have symmetric square roots) is an immediate consequence of its two cited sources. No choice principles are used, consistent with the ZF claim.","at":"2026-07-30T15:04:29.011Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-77-{"id":"thm-three-definitions-of-uniform-space-are-equivalent","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"Verified that [L1], [L2], and [L3] faithfully restate their cited items, and that the proof correctly establishes the ZF equivalence of entourage and uniform-cover formulations via [L1], and the DC-dependent equivalence with gauges via [L2] (every entourage uniformity is generated by a gauge) and [L3] (every gauge generates an entourage uniformity), with the round-trip from entourage to gauge and back recovering the original uniformity by the definition of 'generated by' in [L2].","at":"2026-07-30T15:05:05.016Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-78-{"id":"def-cauchy-filter-in-a-uniform-space","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"The definition correctly states the standard notion of a Cauchy filter on a uniform space: for every entourage E in the uniformity, some filter member A satisfies A×A⊆E. It properly cites def-uniform-space-by-entourages for the notions of uniform space and entourage, and def-filter for the notion of filter. The terminology 'E-small' is introduced consistently. All sibling items on the page that reference this definition (lem-convergent-filters-are-cauchy, lem-cauchy-filter-with-a-cluster-point-converges, def-complete-uniform-space, lem-minimal-cauchy-filter-associated-to-a-cauchy-filter, lem-ultrafilters-on-totally-bounded-uniform-spaces-are-cauchy) restate it faithfully. No defects found.","at":"2026-07-30T15:05:27.507Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-79-{"id":"lem-normal-sequences-of-entourages","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"Verified that step 1.1 correctly derives a symmetric D with D-included-in-E and D-circ3-included-in-E from two applications of [L1] (symmetric square roots), step 2.1 correctly establishes seriality of the relation on symmetric entourages, and step 3.1 correctly applies DC starting at a symmetric E_1 subset U and adjoins E_0 = X×X with all four stated properties (symmetry, E_0=X×X, E_1⊆U, decreasing, E_{n+1}^{∘3}⊆E_n) verified including the n=0 case. [L1] faithfully restates what the cited lemma's proof establishes (symmetric S with S∘S⊆E via S⊆D and D∘D⊆E), and [L2] faithfully restates DC. Cross-references to sibling items are accurate.","at":"2026-07-30T15:05:27.845Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-80-{"id":"lem-convergent-filters-are-cauchy","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"The proof correctly establishes that a convergent filter is Cauchy: step 1.1 chooses a symmetric entourage D with D∘D⊆E (supported by the cited thm-uniformity-induces-a-topology, whose [A1] explicitly asserts symmetric square roots), step 1.2 uses convergence to put D[x] in the filter, step 2.1 verifies E-smallness via symmetry, and step 3.1 discharges the arbitrary entourage. All [L#] facts faithfully restate their cited sources, and the logic is sound.","at":"2026-07-30T15:06:01.984Z"}
--
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-6-{"id":"def-uniformly-continuous-map","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki Definition 4.6 and the BIU notes give the same entourage pullback quantifiers for uniform continuity.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-7-{"id":"thm-uniformly-continuous-maps-are-continuous","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki states exactly that a uniformly continuous map is continuous in the respective uniform topologies; the item supplies the standard ball proof.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-8-{"id":"lem-metric-uniformity-dictionary","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the metric entourages, induced metric topology, separation, and epsilon-delta interpretation. The item packages these clauses. Unlike the source convention, the library permits an empty metric carrier while requiring filters proper; this creates the empty-carrier defect escalated in A3.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-9-{"id":"def-uniform-cover-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"Encyclopedia of Mathematics gives the uniform-cover axioms and Wodzicki develops the associated cover relation. The item uses an equivalent star-refinement presentation and currently overstates equivalence on the empty carrier under the library proper-filter convention.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl:10:{"id":"lem-entourage-and-uniform-cover-dictionary","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the standard equivalence between entourage and uniform-cover formulations. The item spells out both constructions; its cover-to-entourage direction needs the nonempty-carrier qualification recorded for A3.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-11-{"id":"def-gauge-of-pseudometrics","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf","https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources define a uniformity from finite intersections of pseudometric balls. The item calls the family a gauge and bundles topology and uniform-continuity clauses; under the local proper-filter convention it also needs a nonempty carrier.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-12-{"id":"lem-normal-sequences-of-entourages","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Iterated symmetric entourage roots are the standard normal-sequence construction in both sources, but the Statement makes the strictly weaker foundational hypothesis DC explicit and no located source states that same choice-calibrated proposition. It is established, not novel; the recursion proof is local. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: Iterating symmetric entourage roots to obtain a decreasing normal sequence with triple-composite control is the classical uniform-space construction. Exposing DC as the recursion principle is a foundational calibration of that standard result, not a novel claim.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-13-{"id":"lem-pseudometric-from-a-normal-entourage-sequence","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"Both sources state the normal-sequence metrization lemma with the same dyadic control inclusions and path-infimum pseudometric construction. The proof is a notation-adjusted expansion.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-14-{"id":"thm-entourage-uniformities-are-generated-by-gauges","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. The sources prove every uniformity is generated by pseudometrics but assume an ordinary classical background; the item isolates DC as sufficient. The theorem is standard, but no source with the same foundational hypothesis surfaced. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: Representation of an arbitrary uniformity by a family of pseudometrics is a standard uniform-space theorem, and the current proof follows the usual normal-sequence construction. Stating DC as sufficient is an explicit choice calibration, so the local form is established and ai-altered.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
